# Line endings: LF, in every checkout, on every machine.
#
# These games are served from a Linux box and deployed over ssh, so a CRLF is
# either a wasted byte in every response or a shell script that will not run.
#
# This file has to exist rather than relying on a machine setting. PortableGit
# on this machine ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its *system* config, which
# rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout. The working tree then stops
# matching the blob — and since the deployed file IS the working-tree file, the
# live byte count stops matching the repo. Permanently, and silently: git
# normalises on commit, so `git status` reports the tree clean either way.
#
# "Compare the live size with the local size" is the first thing anyone does
# when a deploy looks broken. A permanent false negative there costs a
# debugging session every time it is trusted.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
